<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tuileries Palace</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1564–1871</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Palais des Tuileries</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tuileries Palace was a royal and imperial residence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, begun in 1564 for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-de-medici">Catherine de' Medici</a> beside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a> was brought there from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> in 1789, and its storming on 10 August 1792 brought down the monarchy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> and later rulers lived there until 1870; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communards">Communards</a> set it on fire in May 1871, and the ruins were demolished in 1883, leaving the Tuileries Garden.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tuileries-palace.jpg" alt="Tuileries Palace" width="320" height="245" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gustave Le Gray · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Le_Palais_des_Tuileries_-_Le_Gray.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
